  "query_preview": "..\n\nNext 7 days (Through next Tuesday)... \n\nWater vapor imagery shows vigorous shortwave trough dropping south\nfrom Alberta into Montana. Associated cold front is expected to\npush into east central MT after 3 pm, and slide south through our\nCWA late today through this evening. Scattered showers and \nthunderstorms are expected to increase in coverage late today. \nLatest meso-analysis shows MUCAPE on the order of 100-350 J/kg \nlate today with skinny CAPE sounding profiles. This suggests \ngarden-variety thunderstorms with erratic wind gusts 25-35 mph and\nbrief downpours. Cannot rule out some pea size hail here and \nthere due to relatively low freezing levels. Heads up to those \nwith outdoor plans this evening.\n\nAs main energy in upper trough digs through western MT and ID, a\ncooler air mass and upslope flow settles in over our area tonight.\nSo look for the convective precipitation to transition to a \nwidespread stratiform precipitation event overnight. Cross \nsections show upslope deepens to 600 mb by 12Z Thursday over\nsouth central and foothill locations. Thus, precip will become \nmost widespread over our southern upslope areas, gradually \nshifting eastward Thursday with the upper trough axis. \n\nThe chance of at least 0.50\" of rain are high in these upslope \nareas (>80%) but even Billings-Hardin could see a half inch (>50%\nchance). Other areas north and east of I-90 will see less precip \n(0.05-0.25\"). Some Hi-resolution models are stretching better \nprecip totals northward along the pivoting axis of the upper \ntrough (think eastern Yellowstone into Treasure and Northern \nRosebud), so would not be surprised to see our NBM guidance trend \nthis way. Due to deep upslope and additional dynamic forcing as \naforementioned trough axis pivots around our region Thursday, some\nareas could potentially see over an inch of precipitation. This \nlooks most likely on the north aspects of the \nBeartooths/Pryors/northern Bighorns into southern Yellowstone/Big \nHorn County (20-35% chance).\n\nSnowfall...Snow levels will start out over 8k ft today and drop \nto around 6k ft by 12Z Thursday. North facing slopes of the higher\nelevations may see 8-13 inches in the Beartooths, with the Big \nHorns seeing 5-10 inches through Thursday evening. We have issued\na Winter Storm Warning for the Beartooths from 6 pm this evening \nto 6 pm Thursday...and a Winter Weather Advisory for the Big \nHorns/Pryors from 3 am tonight through 9 pm Thursday evening. The \nBeartooth Hwy is scheduled to open Friday but there may be some \nplowing needed first. We could see some wet snow develop in Red \nLodge by morning, but we expect accumulations to remain around  \nan inch on natural surfaces there, with roads just wet or a \nlittle slushy.\n\nAs the synoptic forcing shifts east Thursday there should be \nenough instability for the development of scattered diurnal \nshowers on the back side of the trough (Wheatland/Golden\nValley/Stillwater/Sweet Grass)...and some thunderstorms are\npossible Thursday afternoon across the MT/Dakota borders in the\nwarm sector ahead of the main trough axis. Precip shifts \neast/southeast by late Thursday evening with the trough axis \npushing into the Dakotas.\n\nFriday through the weekend will trend warmer and be mostly dry, \nbut there may be some weak perturbations aloft to produce \nisolated diurnal convection, at least over the mountains. After \ntemps in the 40s and 50s Thursday...look for temps back to the 60s\nFriday, 70s Saturday and into the 80s Sunday.\n\nThe first half of next week will see the next Pacific trough  \nbegin to move inland. Ensemble and cluster analysis still shows \nhigh spread in potential solutions, ranging from an open wave \nacross the northern Rockies to a deep low forming over the SW \nCONUS...but overall trend seems to favor the latter. If this is\nthe case, its a pattern that would possibly advect low level\nmoisture into our region from the Gulf. For now, look for a warm \nstart to the next work week (80s) then modest cooling and an \nincreasing chance of showers/t-storms each day. BT",
